(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[18] = {"n":18,"set":"B","level":"Intermediate","band":"B1 45% · B2 55%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Thawing permafrost","p":["Across the far north of the planet, in Siberia, Alaska, northern Canada and parts of Scandinavia, lies a vast area of ground that has remained frozen for thousands of years. This permanently frozen ground, called permafrost, covers about a quarter of the land in the Northern Hemisphere. In some places, it is more than a kilometre deep. Today, however, rising global temperatures are causing permafrost to thaw, with consequences that could affect the entire world.","Permafrost is defined as ground that stays at or below zero degrees Celsius for at least two consecutive years. Above it is the 'active layer', a layer of soil that thaws in summer and freezes again in winter. As the Arctic warms, which it is doing about three to four times faster than the global average, the active layer is becoming deeper, and in some areas permafrost is disappearing altogether.","One of the most serious concerns is the release of greenhouse gases. Permafrost contains enormous amounts of organic material, the remains of plants and animals that died long ago but did not decompose because they were frozen. Scientists estimate that permafrost holds about twice as much carbon as is currently in the atmosphere. When the ground thaws, microorganisms begin to break down this material, releasing carbon dioxide and methane, a gas that traps heat much more powerfully than carbon dioxide over short periods. This creates a dangerous feedback loop: warming causes thawing, which releases gases that cause further warming.","Thawing permafrost also causes serious local problems. Much of the infrastructure in Arctic regions, including houses, roads, pipelines and airports, was built on the assumption that the ground would remain frozen and solid. As it thaws, the ground can sink unevenly, damaging buildings and causing roads to crack. In some Arctic communities, homes have had to be abandoned. Coastal erosion is also increasing, as frozen ground that once protected coastlines is washed away by waves.","There are other, less expected risks. In 2016, an outbreak of anthrax, a dangerous bacterial disease, killed thousands of reindeer in Siberia and made several people ill. Scientists believe that a heatwave thawed the remains of an animal that had died of the disease decades earlier, releasing the bacteria. Researchers have also found ancient viruses in permafrost, although most experts believe the risk to humans is low.","Slowing the thawing of permafrost depends mainly on reducing global greenhouse gas emissions. Scientists are monitoring permafrost closely using ground sensors, drilling and satellite images to better understand how quickly it is changing. What happens in these remote, frozen lands may play an important role in shaping the future climate of the whole planet."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of Siberia","how to build houses in cold regions","the causes and effects of thawing permafrost","the life of reindeer"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains permafrost and the consequences of thawing.","h":"أسباب ذوبان التربة الصقيعية وآثاره."},{"n":2,"s":"According to paragraph 2, how is permafrost defined?","o":["ground covered by snow all year","soil that thaws every summer","ice found only under the sea","ground that stays at or below zero degrees for at least two years"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 gives the definition.","h":"تعريف التربة الصقيعية."},{"n":3,"s":"What is the 'active layer'?","o":["the deepest part of permafrost","a layer of ice in the sea","a type of gas","soil above permafrost that thaws in summer and freezes in winter"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Paragraph 2 defines it.","h":"الطبقة النشطة."},{"n":4,"s":"Why has organic material in permafrost not decomposed?","o":["It is made of rock.","It has been frozen.","It is too deep for bacteria.","It contains methane."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"It did not decompose because it was frozen.","h":"لأنها متجمدة."},{"n":5,"s":"What is the 'feedback loop' described in paragraph 3?","o":["Cold weather causes more snow.","Plants grow faster and absorb carbon.","Methane turns into carbon dioxide.","Warming causes thawing, which releases gases that cause more warming."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this cycle.","h":"حلقة التغذية الراجعة."},{"n":6,"s":"Why is thawing permafrost a problem for buildings and roads?","o":["The ground becomes too hard.","Snow covers the roads.","The ground can sink unevenly.","Buildings become too cold."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"هبوط الأرض."},{"n":7,"s":"Why does the writer mention the anthrax outbreak?","o":["as an example of an unexpected risk of thawing","to describe the life of reindeer","to explain how vaccines work","to show that Siberia is very cold"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"It illustrates 'less expected risks'.","h":"مثال على خطر غير متوقع."},{"n":8,"s":"What do most experts believe about ancient viruses in permafrost?","o":["They will cause a global pandemic.","The risk to humans is low.","They are harmless to animals.","They have already infected many people."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 says so.","h":"الخطر منخفض."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way of monitoring permafrost?","o":["using weather balloons","ground sensors","drilling","satellite images"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Balloons are not mentioned.","h":"البالونات غير مذكورة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's main point in the last paragraph?","o":["Permafrost is unimportant to most people.","Scientists cannot study permafrost.","Changes in permafrost may affect the global climate.","Permafrost will soon disappear completely."],"k":2,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"What happens there may shape the planet's future climate.","h":"تأثير عالمي."}]},{"t":"The Rosetta Stone","p":["In the British Museum in London, one object attracts more visitors than almost any other: a dark grey slab of stone, just over a metre tall, covered with ancient writing. It is the Rosetta Stone, and its discovery made it possible, for the first time in more than a thousand years, to read the hieroglyphs of ancient Egypt.","The stone was found in 1799 by French soldiers who were rebuilding a fort near the town of Rashid, known in Europe as Rosetta, in the Nile Delta. The French army, led by Napoleon Bonaparte, had invaded Egypt the year before, bringing with it a team of scholars to study the country. These scholars quickly realized the importance of the stone. After the British defeated the French in Egypt in 1801, the stone was taken to London.","What made the stone so valuable was that it contained the same text written in three different scripts. The top section was written in hieroglyphs, the picture-like script used for important religious and royal texts. The middle section was written in Demotic, an everyday script used by ordinary Egyptians. The bottom section was written in ancient Greek, which scholars could still read. The text was a decree issued in 196 BCE by a council of priests, praising the young king Ptolemy V.","Because scholars could understand the Greek, they hoped to use it to work out the meaning of the hieroglyphs. However, this proved extremely difficult. For centuries, many people had believed that hieroglyphs were purely symbolic, with each sign representing an idea rather than a sound. The English scientist Thomas Young made important progress, showing that some signs inside oval shapes called cartouches represented the sounds of royal names.","The final breakthrough came from the French scholar Jean-François Champollion. He had studied many languages, including Coptic, a later form of the Egyptian language that was still used in church services. Champollion realized that hieroglyphs were a mixture of signs that represented sounds and signs that represented ideas. In 1822, he announced that he had deciphered the system. According to a popular story, he ran into his brother's office shouting 'I've got it!' and then fainted from excitement.","Champollion's achievement opened up the history of ancient Egypt. Scholars could finally read the inscriptions on temples, tombs and papyrus scrolls, learning about Egyptian kings, religion, science and daily life. Today, the phrase 'Rosetta Stone' is used to describe anything that provides the key to understanding a difficult problem. Egypt has also asked for the stone to be returned, and the question of where such objects belong remains an important debate."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Why is the Rosetta Stone so important?","o":["It made it possible to read Egyptian hieroglyphs again.","It is the largest stone in the British Museum.","It was made by Napoleon.","It shows a map of ancient Egypt."],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"Paragraph 1 states this.","h":"مكّن من قراءة الهيروغليفية."},{"n":12,"s":"Who found the Rosetta Stone?","o":["British scholars","Egyptian farmers","French soldiers rebuilding a fort","Thomas Young"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"جنود فرنسيون."},{"n":13,"s":"Why was the stone taken to London?","o":["Napoleon gave it as a gift.","The British defeated the French in Egypt.","Champollion sent it there.","The Egyptians sold it."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"هزيمة الفرنسيين."},{"n":14,"s":"What made the stone especially valuable to scholars?","o":["It was made of gold.","It was the oldest stone in Egypt.","It described how pyramids were built.","It contained the same text in three scripts, including Greek."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"النص نفسه بثلاث كتابات."},{"n":15,"s":"What was Demotic?","o":["a religious script for kings","a form of ancient Greek","a type of stone","an everyday script used by ordinary Egyptians"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 defines it.","h":"الكتابة الشعبية."},{"n":16,"s":"What mistaken belief made deciphering hieroglyphs difficult?","o":["that hieroglyphs were Greek","that the stone was fake","that each sign represented only an idea","that Coptic was unrelated to Egyptian"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"الاعتقاد بأنها رمزية فقط."},{"n":17,"s":"What did Thomas Young discover?","o":["Signs in cartouches represented the sounds of royal names.","The full meaning of every hieroglyph.","The location of the stone.","The Coptic language."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 describes his contribution.","h":"أسماء الملوك صوتية."},{"n":18,"s":"Why was Champollion's knowledge of Coptic useful?","o":["Coptic was written on the stone.","Coptic was the language of Napoleon.","Coptic used the Greek alphabet only.","Coptic was a later form of the Egyptian language."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"مرحلة متأخرة من المصرية."},{"n":19,"s":"The word 'deciphered' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["destroyed","worked out the meaning of","copied","hidden"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"He worked out the writing system.","h":"فك الرموز."},{"n":20,"s":"What does the phrase 'Rosetta Stone' mean today?","o":["a very heavy object","something that provides the key to understanding a problem","a museum in London","an ancient Egyptian king"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"The last paragraph explains the modern meaning.","h":"مفتاح الفهم."}]},{"t":"The science of laughter","p":["Laughter is one of the most familiar human behaviours, found in every culture in the world. We laugh at jokes, funny videos and silly mistakes, but research shows that most laughter has surprisingly little to do with humour. Scientists who study laughter believe that it is, above all, a social signal that helps people connect with each other.","The psychologist Robert Provine spent years observing people laughing in everyday situations, such as shopping centres, offices and university campuses. He found that only about ten to twenty per cent of laughter followed anything that could be described as a joke. Most of it followed ordinary comments such as 'See you later' or 'Are you sure?' He also found that people are about thirty times more likely to laugh when they are with others than when they are alone. Interestingly, speakers laugh more than their listeners, and laughter usually occurs at the end of sentences rather than interrupting them.","Laughter is much older than language. Researchers have found that chimpanzees, gorillas and other great apes produce laughter-like sounds during play, especially when tickling each other. Even rats make high-pitched sounds, too high for humans to hear, when they are tickled. This suggests that laughter first developed as a way of showing that an activity, such as rough play, was friendly rather than aggressive.","Laughter also affects the body. When we laugh, the muscles of the face, chest and stomach contract, and breathing changes. Some studies suggest that laughing releases endorphins, chemicals in the brain that create a feeling of well-being and can reduce pain. In one experiment, people who watched a comedy video were able to tolerate more pain than people who watched a documentary. Laughter may also reduce stress hormones and help people relax.","Laughter is highly contagious. Hearing others laugh activates areas of the brain that prepare us to laugh too, which is why television programmes have sometimes used recorded laughter. Shared laughter strengthens relationships and builds trust, and couples who laugh together tend to report greater satisfaction in their relationships.","However, laughter is not always positive. People can also laugh at others in a cruel way, using it to exclude or embarrass them. The meaning of laughter depends greatly on the situation and on the relationship between the people involved. Understanding laughter, therefore, means understanding not only the brain but also the complex social world in which we live."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Laughter is mainly a social signal that helps people connect.","Laughter is caused mostly by jokes.","Only humans can laugh.","Laughter is always positive."],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"Paragraph 1 states this.","h":"إشارة اجتماعية."},{"n":22,"s":"What did Robert Provine find about most laughter?","o":["It followed funny films.","It happened when people were alone.","It interrupted sentences.","It followed ordinary comments, not jokes."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Only 10–20% followed a joke.","h":"تتبع تعليقات عادية."},{"n":23,"s":"According to Provine, when are people most likely to laugh?","o":["when they are alone","when they are asleep","when they are reading","when they are with others"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"About thirty times more likely with others.","h":"مع الآخرين."},{"n":24,"s":"Why does the writer mention apes and rats?","o":["to compare their intelligence","to show that laughter existed before language","to describe animal experiments in zoos","to explain how tickling works"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Laughter is older than language.","h":"الضحك أقدم من اللغة."},{"n":25,"s":"Why might laughter have first developed?","o":["to frighten enemies","to attract food","to show that play was friendly rather than aggressive","to communicate danger"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"إظهار الود في اللعب."},{"n":26,"s":"What are endorphins?","o":["muscles in the face","chemicals in the brain that create well-being and reduce pain","stress hormones","sounds made by rats"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Paragraph 4 defines them.","h":"مواد كيميائية مريحة."},{"n":27,"s":"What did the comedy video experiment show?","o":["Documentaries are more relaxing.","People who laughed could tolerate more pain.","Comedy increases stress.","Laughter has no effect on the body."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Paragraph 4 reports this.","h":"تحمّل ألم أكبر."},{"n":28,"s":"Why have television programmes used recorded laughter?","o":["It is cheaper than actors.","It hides mistakes.","Hearing laughter makes viewers more likely to laugh.","It makes programmes shorter."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"Laughter is contagious.","h":"الضحك معدٍ."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a possible effect of laughter?","o":["improving eyesight","reducing pain","reducing stress hormones","strengthening relationships"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Eyesight is not mentioned.","h":"البصر غير مذكور."},{"n":30,"s":"What point does the writer make in the last paragraph?","o":["Laughter should be avoided at work.","All laughter is cruel.","The meaning of laughter depends on the social situation.","Scientists fully understand laughter."],"k":2,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"Laughter can also be cruel; its meaning depends on the situation.","h":"المعنى يعتمد على السياق."}]},{"t":"Coffee's journey from Yemen to the world","p":["Coffee is one of the most widely consumed drinks on Earth, with billions of cups drunk every day. Yet its journey from a little-known plant to a global commodity began in the mountains of East Africa and the southern Arabian Peninsula, and much of its early history is closely connected with the Muslim world.","The coffee plant, Coffea arabica, grows naturally in the highlands of Ethiopia. A popular legend tells of a goat herder named Kaldi who noticed that his goats became unusually energetic after eating the red berries of a certain shrub. Although this story is probably not true, it reflects the fact that people in Ethiopia knew about the effects of coffee long before the rest of the world.","The first clear historical evidence of coffee drinking comes from Yemen in the fifteenth century. Sufi scholars and worshippers drank a drink made from roasted coffee beans to help them stay awake during long nights of prayer and remembrance of God. Yemeni farmers began cultivating coffee on terraces in the mountains, and the port of Mocha on the Red Sea became the centre of the world's coffee trade. For about two hundred years, almost all the coffee in the world came from Yemen, and the name 'Mocha' is still used today.","From Yemen, coffee spread quickly to Makkah, Cairo, Damascus and Istanbul. Coffee houses opened in many cities, becoming lively places where people met to talk, play games, listen to poetry and discuss news. Some authorities worried that coffee houses encouraged gossip and political criticism, and there were occasional attempts to ban them, but these did not last long.","In the seventeenth century, coffee reached Europe through trade with the Ottoman Empire, and coffee houses became popular in Venice, London, Paris and Vienna. To protect their trade, Yemeni merchants tried to prevent fertile coffee beans from leaving the country, often by boiling or roasting them before export. However, according to tradition, an Indian pilgrim named Baba Budan smuggled seven beans out of Yemen and planted them in southern India. Later, the Dutch and French established plantations in their colonies in Asia and the Caribbean, and by the eighteenth century, coffee was grown in Brazil, which is now the world's largest producer.","In Saudi Arabia, coffee holds a special cultural place. Arabic coffee, lightly roasted and flavoured with cardamom, is served in small cups as a sign of hospitality and generosity. In 2015, Arabic coffee was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage. The Kingdom is also reviving coffee farming in the mountains of Jazan, continuing a tradition that stretches back centuries."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to make Arabic coffee","the history of coffee and its spread from Yemen","the economy of Brazil","the life of goat herders"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage traces coffee's journey.","h":"تاريخ القهوة وانتشارها."},{"n":32,"s":"What does the story of Kaldi suggest?","o":["People in Ethiopia knew about coffee's effects early.","Goats were the first to drink coffee.","Coffee was invented in Yemen.","Kaldi was a famous merchant."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"The legend reflects early Ethiopian knowledge.","h":"معرفة مبكرة في الحبشة."},{"n":33,"s":"Why did Sufi scholars in Yemen drink coffee?","o":["to stay awake during long nights of prayer","to cure illnesses","to celebrate weddings","to trade with Europe"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"للسهر في العبادة."},{"n":34,"s":"Why is the name 'Mocha' still used today?","o":["It is the name of a coffee plant.","It is the largest city in Yemen.","It was a famous coffee house in London.","Mocha was once the centre of the world's coffee trade."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"مركز تجارة القهوة."},{"n":35,"s":"According to paragraph 4, why did some authorities want to ban coffee houses?","o":["Coffee was too expensive.","Coffee was bad for health.","They worried about gossip and political criticism.","Coffee houses were too noisy at night."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"القلق من النقد السياسي."},{"n":36,"s":"Why did Yemeni merchants boil or roast beans before export?","o":["to stop other countries from growing coffee","to improve the flavour","to make them lighter","to kill insects"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"They tried to prevent fertile beans leaving the country.","h":"منع زراعتها خارج اليمن."},{"n":37,"s":"The word 'smuggled' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["bought openly","took secretly and illegally","cooked","planted"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Baba Budan took the beans secretly.","h":"هرّب."},{"n":38,"s":"Which country is now the world's largest coffee producer?","o":["Yemen","India","Brazil","Ethiopia"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 states this.","h":"البرازيل."},{"n":39,"s":"What does Arabic coffee represent in Saudi culture?","o":["hospitality and generosity","wealth and power","religious study","trade with Europe"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"The last paragraph explains this.","h":"الضيافة والكرم."},{"n":40,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["as a comparison of tea and coffee","as a list of health benefits","as an argument against coffee","chronologically and by place, following coffee's spread"],"k":3,"ty":"organization","e":"Ethiopia → Yemen → Middle East → Europe → world → Saudi Arabia.","h":"ترتيب زمني ومكاني."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"I'm tired because I ______ for the exam all night.","o":["revise","am revising since","have been revising","have revise"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have been revising — Recent continuous activity with a present result.","h":"المضارع التام المستمر."},{"n":42,"s":"The road ______ at the moment, so we'll have to take another route.","o":["is repairing","is being repaired","repairs","has repaired"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is being repaired — Present continuous passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول المستمر."},{"n":43,"s":"She ______ be at home. I saw her at the shopping centre five minutes ago.","o":["must","can't","should","might"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"can't — Logical impossibility.","h":"استنتاج النفي."},{"n":44,"s":"This is the house in ______ the famous poet lived.","o":["which","that","where","whom"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — preposition + which.","h":"in which."},{"n":45,"s":"If you ______ so fast, you wouldn't have had the accident.","o":["didn't drive","wouldn't drive","haven't driven","hadn't driven"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"hadn't driven — Third conditional.","h":"الشرط الثالث."},{"n":46,"s":"She insisted on ______ for the meal.","o":["to pay","pay","paying","paid"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"paying — insist on + -ing.","h":"insist on + -ing."},{"n":47,"s":"He got his phone screen ______ at a small shop near his house.","o":["fix","fixed","fixing","to fix"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"fixed — Causative: get + object + past participle.","h":"التركيب السببي."},{"n":48,"s":"This year's results are ______ better than last year's.","o":["very","too","most","considerably"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"considerably — Adverb + comparative: considerably better.","h":"considerably مع المقارنة."},{"n":49,"s":"The ground is wet. It ______ have rained during the night.","o":["can't","needn't","shouldn't","must"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"must — Logical certainty about the past.","h":"استنتاج يقيني."},{"n":50,"s":"I wish I ______ able to come to your party, but I'll be abroad.","o":["am","will be","were","had been"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — Present/future wish: wish + were.","h":"wish + were."},{"n":51,"s":"This time tomorrow, we ______ over the Mediterranean.","o":["will be flying","fly","will have flown","are flown"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will be flying — Future continuous.","h":"المستقبل المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"My mother reminded me ______ my passport before leaving for the airport.","o":["to take","taking","take","that take"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to take — remind + object + to + verb.","h":"remind + to."},{"n":53,"s":"______ his lack of experience, he was offered the job.","o":["Although","Despite","Because","However"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Despite — Despite + noun phrase.","h":"Despite."},{"n":54,"s":"There were ______ people at the concert that we couldn't move.","o":["such","so much","too many that","so many"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"so many — so many + countable noun + that.","h":"so many."},{"n":55,"s":"The furniture in the new office ______ very modern.","o":["is","are","were","have been"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is — furniture is uncountable and singular.","h":"furniture مفرد."},{"n":56,"s":"When we lived near the sea, we ______ swim every morning before school.","o":["were","are used to","would","use"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would — would for repeated past actions.","h":"would للعادة."},{"n":57,"s":"The two brothers, both of ______ are engineers, built their own house.","o":["whom","who","them","which"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whom — both of whom.","h":"both of whom."},{"n":58,"s":"Only then ______ how serious the situation was.","o":["we understood","did we understand","we did understand","understood we"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did we understand — Only then: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Only then."},{"n":59,"s":"We can't risk ______ the last train.","o":["missing","to miss","miss","missed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"missing — risk + -ing.","h":"risk + -ing."},{"n":60,"s":"Provided that you ______ the instructions, the machine is easy to use.","o":["will follow","followed","follow","would follow"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"follow — provided that + present for a future condition.","h":"provided that."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"My (A) grandfather (B) has lived in this house (C) since fifty (D) years.","o":["grandfather","has lived","since","years"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"since — for + a period: for fifty years.","h":"for مع المدة."},{"n":62,"s":"(A) Do your brother (B) work (C) in the (D) hospital?","o":["Do","work","in","hospital"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Do — your brother is singular: Does.","h":"Does مع المفرد."},{"n":63,"s":"If it (A) rains tomorrow, we (B) will (C) stay at (D) the home.","o":["rains","will","stay","the"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"the — Fixed expression: at home (no article).","h":"at home دون the."},{"n":64,"s":"The (A) students (B) were (C) exciting (D) about the trip.","o":["students","were","exciting","about"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"exciting — Their feeling: excited.","h":"excited."},{"n":65,"s":"She (A) told me that she (B) had (C) already (D) finish the report.","o":["told","had","already","finish"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"finish — Past perfect: had finished.","h":"التصريف الثالث."},{"n":66,"s":"He (A) enjoys (B) to swim (C) in the (D) sea.","o":["enjoys","to swim","in","sea"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to swim — enjoy + -ing: swimming.","h":"enjoy + -ing."},{"n":67,"s":"The building (A) which was (B) damaged in the storm (C) is being (D) repair.","o":["which","damaged","is","repair"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"repair — Passive: is being repaired.","h":"التصريف الثالث."},{"n":68,"s":"Neither of the (A) answers (B) were (C) correct, (D) unfortunately.","o":["answers","were","correct","unfortunately"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"were — Neither of … takes a singular verb in formal English: was.","h":"Neither of مع المفرد."},{"n":69,"s":"Not only (A) did she (B) win the race, (C) she also broke (D) the record.","o":["did","win","she also","the"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"she also — Not only … but also: but she also broke.","h":"but also."},{"n":70,"s":"(A) There (B) is many reasons (C) why people (D) move to cities.","o":["There","is","why","move"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"is — many reasons is plural: There are.","h":"There are مع الجمع."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M18-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"Why was the woman charged twice?","o":["She used too much data.","She paid late.","There was a system error.","She changed her plan."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'there was a system error last week'.","h":"خطأ في النظام."}],"t":"A duplicate charge on a bill","sc":["Situation: A customer calls her mobile phone company. (63 words)","Woman: Hello. I've just checked my bill, and I've been charged twice for the same month.","Man: Let me look at your account. You're right, there was a system error last week that affected some customers.","Woman: So will I get my money back?","Man: Yes. The extra amount will be returned to your account within five working days, and we'll send you a message to confirm."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M18-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is the main problem with the slides?","o":["They have no title.","They contain too much text.","They have too many images.","They are in the wrong order."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'there's a lot of text on each slide'.","h":"نص كثير."}],"t":"Asking a classmate to review slides","sc":["Situation: Two students are preparing for a presentation. (62 words)","Man: Could you look at my slides before tomorrow's presentation? I'm not sure they're clear.","Man: Sure. Hmm, there's a lot of text on each slide. The audience won't be able to read it all while you're talking.","Man: So should I remove some?","Man: Yes. Keep only the key points, and add a few images or a chart. Say the details instead of writing them."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M18-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"How will the supervisor help the students?","o":["by cancelling the exam","by moving them to another room","by adding ten minutes at the end","by giving them the answers"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll add ten minutes at the end.'","h":"إضافة عشر دقائق."}],"t":"A power cut during an exam","sc":["Situation: An exam supervisor speaks to students. (60 words)","Woman: Please stay calm. The power has gone off, but there's enough light from the windows. Keep working.","Man: Excuse me, will we get extra time?","Woman: Yes. We've lost about ten minutes because of the confusion, so I'll add ten minutes at the end.","Man: Thank you. And the air conditioning?","Woman: Maintenance staff are working on it now. It should be back soon."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M18-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why have the books been delayed?","o":["The school gave the wrong address.","There have been new customs checks at the port.","The books are out of print.","The supplier forgot the order."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'There's been a delay at the port because of new customs checks.'","h":"إجراءات الجمارك."}],"t":"A delayed shipment of books","sc":["Situation: A school librarian calls a book supplier. (67 words)","Woman: Hello, I'm calling from Al-Noor School. We ordered two hundred books for our library a month ago, and they still haven't arrived.","Man: I apologize. There's been a delay at the port because of new customs checks.","Woman: When can we expect them? We're opening the new reading room next week.","Man: They should arrive on Thursday. If they don't, we'll send the books we have in our warehouse immediately."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M18-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"Why do they choose the seafood restaurant?","o":["The client prefers fish, and it is quiet.","It is the closest to the office.","It is the cheapest.","The client has been there before."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"He prefers fish; it's quiet for discussion.","h":"يفضل السمك والمكان هادئ."}],"t":"Choosing a restaurant for a client","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are planning a business lunch. (61 words)","Man: We need to take the client from Japan to lunch on Monday. Any suggestions?","Woman: What about the new steak restaurant near the office?","Man: He mentioned that he doesn't eat much meat. He prefers fish.","Woman: Then the seafood restaurant on the corniche would be better. It's quiet, so we can talk about the contract.","Man: Good. I'll book a table for one o'clock."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M18-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Why does the guest want to change rooms?","o":["His room is too small.","His room has no view.","His room is noisy because of traffic.","His air conditioner is broken."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"The room faces the main road.","h":"ضوضاء المرور."}],"t":"A noisy road outside a hotel","sc":["Situation: A guest speaks to a hotel receptionist. (62 words)","Man: Good morning. I couldn't sleep last night. My room faces the main road, and the traffic noise continued until three in the morning.","Woman: I'm sorry to hear that. We have a room on the other side of the building, facing the garden. It's much quieter.","Man: Can I move today?","Woman: Yes. It'll be ready after twelve. I'll ask someone to move your luggage."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M18-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What can the woman do if she checks out at twelve?","o":["leave her luggage and use the hotel facilities","stay in her room for free","get a free taxi","change her flight"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"She can leave her luggage and use the pool and lounge.","h":"تترك أمتعتها وتستخدم المرافق."}],"t":"Asking for a late check-out","sc":["Situation: A guest calls the hotel reception. (66 words)","Woman: Hello, this is Room 512. My flight isn't until nine tonight. Is it possible to check out late?","Man: Normally, check-out is at twelve. We can extend it to four o'clock for an extra fee of one hundred riyals.","Woman: And if I check out at twelve?","Man: Then you're welcome to leave your luggage with us and use the pool and lounge until you leave for the airport."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M18-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What must the student attach to the form?","o":["a copy of his passport","his old certificate","a photo","a letter from his teacher Longer conversations"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'attach a copy of your passport'.","h":"نسخة الجواز."}],"t":"A spelling error on a certificate","sc":["Situation: A student speaks to a university office worker. (66 words)","Man: Excuse me, my name is spelled wrongly on my graduation certificate. It says 'Mohamad' with one 'm' in the middle, but it should be 'Mohammad'.","Woman: I'm sorry about that. Please fill in this correction form and attach a copy of your passport.","Man: How long will it take to get a new certificate?","Woman: About two weeks. We'll send you a text message when it's ready to collect."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M18-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why will the children use a visual programming tool?","o":["It is the only tool available.","Their parents requested it.","It is faster than typing for adults.","Most of them have never written code."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"They have probably never written code.","h":"مبتدئون."},{"n":80,"s":"Why will the students prepare a printed guide for parents?","o":["to advertise the university","so the children can continue coding at home","to explain the workshop fee","to collect feedback"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"'so the children can continue at home'.","h":"لمواصلة التعلم في البيت."}],"t":"Planning a coding workshop","sc":["Situation: Two university students are planning a workshop for school children. (143 words)","Woman: We've been asked to run a coding workshop for twenty children aged ten to twelve. Any ideas?","Woman: Most of them have probably never written code, so we should use a visual programming tool where they drag blocks instead of typing.","Woman: Good idea. What should they make?","Woman: Something fun, like a simple game where a character collects stars. They'll feel proud if they can play it at the end.","Woman: How long should the workshop be?","Woman: Three hours, with a break in the middle. Children that age can't concentrate much longer.","Woman: We should also have a few helpers, so no child gets stuck for too long.","Woman: I'll ask two of our classmates. And let's prepare a printed guide for the parents, so the children can continue at home.","Woman: Excellent. I'll book the computer lab for the second Saturday of next month, from nine until twelve."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M18-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What problem do the volunteers face?","o":["There are not enough donations.","The food bank is closing.","Volunteers do not want to work in Ramadan.","Most volunteers can only come in the evening, but deliveries are in the afternoon."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Volunteers come after iftar, but deliveries are earlier.","h":"توقيت المتطوعين لا يناسب التوصيل."},{"n":82,"s":"What will volunteers do in the evenings?","o":["deliver food","cook iftar meals","collect money","sort donations and pack boxes"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'volunteers can sort donations and pack boxes for the next day'.","h":"فرز التبرعات وتعبئة الصناديق."}],"t":"Discussing a volunteering schedule","sc":["Situation: Two volunteers at a food bank are talking. (152 words)","Man: We need to organize the volunteering schedule for Ramadan. That's when we're busiest.","Man: How many volunteers do we have?","Man: About forty, but many of them can only come in the evenings after iftar.","Man: The problem is that most deliveries happen in the afternoon, before people break their fast.","Man: Exactly. So we need more people in the afternoon. Maybe university students who finish classes early could help.","Man: Good idea. I'll contact the student volunteering club. What about the evenings?","Man: In the evenings, volunteers can sort donations and pack boxes for the next day.","Man: That makes sense. I'll create an online form so volunteers can choose their shifts.","Man: Perfect. Let's send it out by the end of this week, so that we have time to find more people if some shifts are still empty. Last year, we were short of drivers in the final ten days.","Man: Then I'll ask specifically for volunteers with cars."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M18-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why don't they buy ten digital microscopes?","o":["They don't need microscopes.","Digital microscopes are not available.","It would use almost the whole budget.","The inspector said not to."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Ten would use almost the whole budget.","h":"تستهلك الميزانية كلها تقريبًا."},{"n":84,"s":"Why do they want to buy new goggles?","o":["The inspector said the old ones are too old.","Students lost them.","They are part of a competition.","They are cheaper than microscopes. Talks and lectures"],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'The inspector said our goggles are too old.'","h":"المفتش قال إنها قديمة."}],"t":"Choosing equipment for a school lab","sc":["Situation: Two science teachers are discussing a budget. (144 words)","Woman: We have twenty thousand riyals to spend on new equipment for the science lab.","Woman: We really need new microscopes. Half of the old ones don't work properly.","Woman: I agree, but digital microscopes are expensive. Ten of them would use almost the whole budget.","Woman: What if we bought six digital microscopes and repaired some of the old ones?","Woman: That's a good compromise. Then we'd still have money for safety equipment, like new goggles and a fire blanket.","Woman: Safety must come first. The inspector said our goggles are too old.","Woman: Right. Let's prepare a list and get prices from two suppliers before we decide.","Woman: I'll call the suppliers tomorrow and ask about discounts for schools. Some companies also offer free training for teachers when you buy new equipment.","Woman: That would be useful. Ask them about delivery times too. We want everything ready before the new term."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M18-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"What does the lateral line help sharks detect?","o":["the smell of blood","electrical fields","movements and vibrations in the water","light in deep water"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"The lateral line detects movements and vibrations.","h":"الحركة والاهتزاز."},{"n":86,"s":"Why does the lecturer compare sharks' eyes to cats' eyes?","o":["Both are very large.","Both have a reflective layer that helps in low light.","Both can see colours well.","Both are found on the sides of the head."],"k":1,"ty":"purpose of detail","e":"Both have a reflective layer behind the retina.","h":"طبقة عاكسة."},{"n":87,"s":"How can sharks find fish hidden under the sand?","o":["by hearing their heartbeats","by smelling the sand","by using sunlight","by detecting their weak electrical fields"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"The ampullae of Lorenzini detect electrical fields.","h":"المجال الكهربائي."}],"t":"Lecture: how sharks sense their prey","sc":["Situation: A marine biology lecturer speaks to students. (243 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Sharks have been successful hunters for more than four hundred million years, longer than trees have existed on Earth. Part of their success comes from a remarkable set of senses that allow them to find prey in the vast ocean. The first is smell. Some sharks can detect one drop of blood in a large swimming pool of water, and they can tell which nostril the smell reached first, helping them to follow it towards its source. The second is hearing. Sharks are especially sensitive to low-frequency sounds, such as those made by injured or struggling fish, and can hear them from hundreds of metres away. Third, sharks have a special system called the lateral line, a series of tiny canals along each side of the body that detect movements and vibrations in the water. This allows them to sense other animals nearby, even in dark or cloudy water. As the shark gets closer, vision becomes important. Many sharks have eyes adapted to low light, with a reflective layer behind the retina, similar to a cat's eyes. Finally, and perhaps most remarkably, sharks can detect the weak electrical fields produced by the muscles and nerves of other animals. They do this using small pores on their heads, called the ampullae of Lorenzini. This sense is so sensitive that sharks can find fish hidden under the sand. Despite their reputation, sharks rarely attack humans, and many species are now threatened by overfishing."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M18-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"According to the speaker, why is a clear revision plan helpful?","o":["It makes the work feel more manageable.","It guarantees high marks.","It reduces the number of exams.","It replaces the need for sleep."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'the work feels more manageable'.","h":"يجعل العمل أسهل إدارة."},{"n":89,"s":"What does the speaker suggest doing with negative thoughts?","o":["ignoring all thoughts","replacing them with more balanced statements","writing them in a diary","telling them to classmates"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Replace them with balanced statements.","h":"استبدالها بعبارات متوازنة."},{"n":90,"s":"What is the purpose of the breathing technique?","o":["to improve memory","to help students sleep longer","to finish exams faster","to calm the body quickly"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"'Repeating this a few times can calm your body quickly'.","h":"التهدئة."}],"t":"Talk: managing exam stress","sc":["Situation: A school psychologist speaks to students. (237 words)","Lecturer: Good morning, everyone. As final exams approach, many of you are feeling stressed. A little stress is normal and can even help you focus, but too much can make it harder to think clearly and remember information. Today, I'd like to share some practical strategies. First, plan your revision. Make a realistic timetable that covers all your subjects, with specific goals for each session. When you can see a clear plan, the work feels more manageable, and you're less likely to panic. Second, look after your body. Try to sleep seven to nine hours a night, eat regular meals and drink plenty of water. Physical activity, even a twenty-minute walk, reduces stress hormones and improves mood. Third, watch your thoughts. Many students tell themselves things like 'I'm going to fail' or 'I'm not good enough'. These thoughts increase anxiety. Try to replace them with more balanced statements, such as 'I've prepared well, and I'll do my best.' Fourth, learn a simple breathing technique. Breathe in slowly through your nose for four seconds, hold for four seconds, then breathe out through your mouth for six seconds. Repeating this a few times can calm your body quickly, even in the exam room. Finally, don't keep your worries to yourself. Talk to your family, friends or teachers, or come and see me. Remember that your exam results are important, but they do not define your worth as a person."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Some computers are very expensive.","It is no surprise that technology has become such a popular career choice.","Many offices are in large cities.","Programming languages change often."],"x":["Many young people now choose careers in technology. These jobs are well paid, offer opportunities to work from anywhere and allow people to create products used by millions. Demand for skilled workers is also growing rapidly. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A conclusion drawn from the reasons.","h":"خلاصة."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Bees play an essential role in agriculture. (2) They pollinate many of the crops that humans eat. (3) Without them, the production of fruits and nuts would fall sharply. (4) Honey is often used in traditional medicine. (5) Unfortunately, bee populations are declining in many countries."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Honey in medicine does not develop bees' role in agriculture.","h":"العسل في الطب خارج الفكرة."},{"n":93,"s":"The government (A) has announced that the new law (B) will come into force (C) in next (D) January.","o":["has announced","will come","in","January"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"No preposition before next: next January.","h":"لا حرف جر قبل next."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Clear rules are needed so that scooters can be used safely.","Scooters are usually electric.","Pavements are for walking.","Some cities have many parks."],"x":["Electric scooters have become popular in many cities. They are cheap, convenient and good for short journeys. However, riders sometimes leave them on pavements, where they block pedestrians, and accidents have increased. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A balanced conclusion with a recommendation.","h":"خلاصة متوازنة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Learning history helps us understand the present. (2) It shows how past decisions shaped today's societies. (3) It also teaches us to evaluate evidence carefully. (4) Some history books are very long. (5) Finally, history helps us appreciate different cultures."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"The length of books does not support the value of history.","h":"طول الكتب خارج الموضوع."},{"n":96,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) Then the juice is heated to remove harmful bacteria.","(2) Making orange juice in a factory involves several steps.","(3) First, the oranges are washed and sorted.","(4) Next, machines squeeze the fruit to extract the juice."],"k":0,"ty":"best order","e":"topic → First → Next → Then.","h":"ترتيب الخطوات."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Remote work has both advantages and disadvantages.","Many people work in offices.","Travel can be expensive.","Some cities are very large."],"x":["______ Workers save time and money on travel, and companies can hire talented people from different cities. However, some employees feel isolated."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The paragraph presents both sides.","h":"مزايا وعيوب."},{"n":98,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","Therefore","Nevertheless","In contrast"],"x":["The weather forecast predicted rain. ______, we took our umbrellas."],"k":1,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"Taking umbrellas is the result.","h":"النتيجة."},{"n":99,"s":"She (A) has been (B) living in Jeddah (C) since she (D) has moved there in 2015.","o":["has","living","since","has moved"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"since + past simple: since she moved.","h":"since مع الماضي البسيط."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some cities are near the sea.","Lorries carry goods to shops.","Many people walk to work.","Much of it comes from cars and lorries. Teacher Section — Mock 18"],"x":["Air pollution is a serious problem in many large cities. ______ To reduce it, some cities have introduced low-emission zones where older vehicles are not allowed."],"k":3,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"The source of pollution leads to the solution.","h":"المصدر قبل الحل."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
